{"ex-405-1":{"number":"1","title":"Direction of magnetic field","question":"<p>(a) A long straight wire carries a current into the page (Fig a). Find the direction of magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) A long straight wire carries a current upwards (Fig b). Find the direction of magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) A solenoid carries a current as shown (Fig c).</p><p>(i) Find the direction of magnetic field inside the solenoid.</p><p>(ii) Which end of the solenoid is the S-pole?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4a32df6534e99863963049cd.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:486.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Upwards</p><p>(b) Into page</p><p>(c) (i) Towards right</p><p>(ii) The left end</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/23bbe26299f2f8ef43e7eb29.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:179.4px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/44cdc2df665c8a1bedd48ca5.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:179.4px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e096822e763aea2427f7152d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:182.5px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":2},"id":"ex-405-1","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-2":{"number":"2","title":"Direction of resultant magnetic field","question":"<p>Two parallel straight wires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> carry currents in opposite directions. Find the direction of the resultant magnetic field at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/eef957d29057eaa08e3096ca.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:223.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, both the magnetic fields due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> point upwards.</p><p>∴ The resultant magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> points upwards.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":20},"id":"ex-405-2","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-3":{"number":"3","title":"Electromagnets","question":"<p>A student connects a solenoid to a battery to make an electromagnet (Fig a).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5f0b42e38a5046c66c1dd141.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:366.0px\"/></p><p>(a) How does the strength of the electromagnet change when the student reduces the resistance of the variable resistor?</p><p>(b) Suggest an advantage of an electromagnet over a permanent magnet.</p><p>(c) Both a soft-iron core and a steel core can strengthen the electromagnet. However, after the switch is opened, the steel core remains magnetized (i.e. behaves like a magnet) but the soft-iron core does not. Which core is more suitable in making an electromagnet? Explain.</p><p>(d) The student connects another solenoid in series to the original one (Fig b). The two solenoids are identical and their resistances are negligible. How does the strength of the electromagnet change? Explain.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) When the resistance decreases, the current through the solenoid increases. Therefore, the strength of the electromagnet increases.</p><p>(b) It can be turned off. / Its strength can be varied.</p><p>(c) The soft-iron core is more suitable. If the steel core is used, the electromagnet cannot be turned off.</p><p>(d) The strength remains unchanged because the number of turns per unit length of the solenoid remains unchanged.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 17","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":27},"id":"ex-405-3","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-4":{"number":"4","title":"Electric bell","question":"<p>An electric bell (Fig a) makes use of an electromagnet. Figure b shows the schematic diagram of an electric bell. Explain how it works.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7700984052b5a98d7eace606.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:526.6px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b131af236c8e7be57dd9228d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:209.6px\"/></p><p>When the switch is pressed, the circuit becomes closed. The iron sheet attached to the hammer is attracted by the electromagnet, so the hammer hits the gong. This breaks the connection and the circuit becomes open (Fig c). As a result, the electromagnet loses its magnetic effect. The hammer springs back until it touches the contact-breaker. The circuit is closed again and the whole process is repeated as long as the switch is pressed.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":40},"id":"ex-405-4","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-5":{"number":"5","title":"Distance from wire","question":"<p>A current of 0.5 A passes through a long straight wire. Point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from the wire and the magnetic field there has a strength of</p><p>4 μT. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Take μ<sub>0</sub> = 4π × 10<sup>–7</sup> T m A<sup>–1</sup>.</p>","answer":"<p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p>4 × 10<sup>–6</sup> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.025 m</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":47},"id":"ex-405-5","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-6":{"number":"6","title":"The neutral point between two wires","question":"<p>Two parallel long wires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> carry currents of 2 A and 3 A respectively into the page. They are 6 cm apart.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4be614614404f0f015eae9b6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:228.0px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a neutral point between wires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Find the distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from wire <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the magnetic field due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>) points downwards and the magnetic field due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>) points upwards. They cancel each other out.</p><p>Therefore, by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1d1ada6095a7d6092e330998.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:177.1px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3(0.06 − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.036 m</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>If the current in X increases, how will the position of the neutral point change?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>It moves towards Y, the wire whose current remains unchanged. If the separation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is measured from Y, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Increasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> decreases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":55},"id":"ex-405-6","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-7":{"number":"7","title":"Calculating resultant magnetic field","question":"<p>Two long straight wires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are perpendicular to each other. They carry</p><p>currents of 5 A and 6 A as shown. Take μ<sub>0</sub> = 4π × 10<sup>–7</sup> T m A<sup>–1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Find the resultant magnetic field at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) The measured value of the magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is smaller than the answer in (a). Suggest a reason.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/49548e6fd528b0ae2d58fc6e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:158.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.5 × 10<sup>−5</sup> T (out of page)</p><p>Magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4 × 10<sup>−5</sup> T (into page)</p><p>∴ Resultant magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4 × 10<sup>–5</sup> – 2.5 × 10<sup>–5</sup> = 1.5 × 10<sup>–5</sup> T (into page)</p><p>(b) The Earth’s magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> points out of the page.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":72},"id":"ex-405-7","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-8":{"number":"8","title":"Estimating the permeability of free space","question":"<p>Dick connects a long straight wire as shown (Fig a). He puts a smartphone with a magnetic-field-detector app at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from the wire and measures the magnetic field perpendicular to the page <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Then he varies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and measures the corresponding values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure b shows his results. The ammeter reading is 5 A.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/55a8a6647aed2ad940942587.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:218.5px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f1cb53679d6dc35951e000df.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:337.0px\"/></p><p>(a) Why does the graph not pass through the origin?</p><p>(b) Estimate the value of μ<sub>0</sub> from the graph.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The Earth’s magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> contributes to the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) The magnetic field produced by the current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>∴ Slope of graph = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>μ<sub>0</sub> = 1.11 × 10<sup>–6</sup> T m A<sup>–1</sup></p><p>Revision 5 Q4 (p.213)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 23","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":84},"id":"ex-405-8","chapter":"405"},"ex-405-9":{"number":"9","title":"Magnetic field inside a long solenoid","question":"<p>A current of 2 A is flowing through a long solenoid having a length of</p><p>25 cm and 200 turns. Take μ<sub>0</sub> = 4π × 10<sup>−7</sup> T m A<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Find the strength of the magnetic field near the centre inside the solenoid.</p><p>(b) Explain how the answer in (a) is affected if</p><p>(i) the length and the number of turns are both doubled;</p><p>(ii) the cross-sectional area is reduced by half,</p><p>(iii) the current through the solenoid is doubled.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>μ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>π</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 2.01 × 10<sup>−3</sup> T</p><p>(b) (i) There is no change as the number of turns per unit length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> remains the same.</p><p>(ii) There is no change as the magnetic field inside a long solenoid is independent of the cross-sectional area.</p><p>(iii) Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ∝ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> will be doubled when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is doubled.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 405  |  Source PDF p. 25","source":{"name":"405 Examples.docx","sha256":"68ac838691ab05d7d6916dda1df3ceb28c90e9e53be10cfd1f543628515d1c53","block":100},"id":"ex-405-9","chapter":"405"}}